WEBVTT

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:53.440
This land of ours, its mountains and valleys, fields and forests, a place to live, a place to work, a place to enjoy.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.720
But it's much more than that.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.680
Our landscape teaches us things as well.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:09.360
All around us, there's a vast repository of knowledge and every day brings surprising new discoveries.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:16.360
From Darwin to robot farmers, science and our landscape have gone hand-in-hand.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:25.680
In this edition of Countryfile, we'll be looking at how our landscape has shaped science, and how science has shaped our landscape.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.360
And where better than this, Wytham Woods in Oxfordshire.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:36.200
It's probably the most studied stretch of woodland anywhere in the world, and I'll be catching up with scientists who have turned Wytham Woods into a living laboratory.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:45.320
And we'll be looking back at some of the best science to have featured on Countryfile.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.520
Like the time Matt got into a tight spot, beloved of an 18th century naturalist.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:59.720
And he believed the more confined your sphere of observation, the more perfect would be your remarks.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:09.120
Or when Julia discovered the ancient life hidden in rocks.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:13.160
This is the one we found this morning on the beach.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.520
And...

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.000
JULIA GASPS Look at that!

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.200
There's your ammonite.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.000
And what happened when Adam came face-to-face with the future of farming.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:31.360
When you think of pig farming, you just think of, you know, smelly pigs and perhaps sausages and bacon, but this technology is just extraordinary.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.560
Wytham Woods are owned by Oxford University.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.480
There have been experiments going on here for 70 years.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.520
The woods themselves sit just a few miles west of the city of Oxford.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:03:02.640
It was in these woods that a scientist called Charles Elton made some of his most important discoveries.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:15.240
He had a lifelong passion for national history, and his work here in the 1940s and '50s put the science of ecology on the map.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:22.920
Today, people like Clive Hambler are following in his footsteps, often with nothing more high-tech than water and a nozzle.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:33.360
What I'm trying to do is see if I can find any spiders' webs, and the easiest way to do it is often to make them stand out when they're covered in mist.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.960
Why are you looking for the spiders and their webs?

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:41.720
Because it turns out that these dead plant stems are often teeming with life, and this is one of Charles Elton's great insights.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.120
That the dead material is more important than living material to many of the organisms here.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:54.360
So many people think that animals depend very much on green plants, but in fact, they often depend on them when they're dead.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.640
Back in the 1940s, these ideas were revolutionary.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.120
They sealed Elton's reputation as the foremost ecologist of his day.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:10.440
His was a different way of looking at the world, which is still throwing up challenging ideas.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:24.320
Wildlife often depends on having a very large amount of surface to live on, and it needs a damp environment to live in, so a physically complex thing like a tree, a conifer tree, with lots of little needles, provides a home for lots of wildlife.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.000
And so conifers are actually amongst our best habitats for many types of animal.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.400
A lot of people don't particularly like conifers.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.440
We have plantations of them which get chopped down and people don't want to put any more in their place.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.360
Absolutely.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:47.120
But, if you look at the habitats in Britain that have the most animals and most species per square metre, it's conifer woodland, not oak woodland.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.320
So, this is the kind of inheritance of Elton, to make people think again about...

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.840
That's right.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.040
..About what are often dismissed as, you know, unnecessary things in the countryside?

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.240
That's right.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:14.360
I mean, he started to look at the world from a very general ecological point of view, so he looked at these structures, he looked at the temperature and the rainfall and the moisture in the air, and he realised that all those things come together, and an animal has its niche, or a plant has its niche, which is how it fits into the ecosystem.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:20.280
And one of Elton's great insights was really understanding what a niche was, and defining what a niche was.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:32.640
Charles Elton continued his work in Wytham Woods right up until the late-1960s.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:40.920
His ideas set the tone for scientific study of the environment, and brought about a revolution in thinking about our landscape.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:47.760
Just as, over a century earlier, there had been another huge shift in scientific thinking.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.200
But that wasn't down to academia.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:56.280
It was down to an ordinary girl with an extraordinary passion, as Julia found out when she went to Dorset.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:02.800
To the seaside town of Lyme Regis, the home of British fossil hunting.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:11.720
This beach on the outskirts of town is one of the best places in the world to find fossils.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:14.040
It's jam-packed with them.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:19.000
They're all around me!

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:30.800
These cliffs were created by layers of sediment, deposited 150 to 200 million years ago, trapping dead sea creatures and preserving them as fossils.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:39.640
Anyone's allowed to pick up fossils from the beach, but it's easier with a guide, like fossil hunter Paddy Howe from the Lyme Regis Museum.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:40.040
So, what are we looking for along here?

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.320
I'm looking for stones that might have ammonites inside.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.560
They're flat, grey, fairly smooth.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.480
The edges are either almost sharp like that, or they're flat.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.480
They're not rounded, like most pebbles.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.960
What we can do is split the stone...

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.240
Expertly done!

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.320
Well, there is a small one inside.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.400
Oh, yes, a little baby one!

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.520
I was hoping for something better than that.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.160
Right.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.040
But, er...

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.120
Are going to throw this back to the ocean?

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.360
I'll leave that one, and we'll see if we can find some more.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.640
Come on, then.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.960
Absolutely nothing.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.720
No!

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.760
Rubbish!

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.080
What kind of a fossil hunter are you?!

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:27.480
'Mmm, maybe it's not so easy after all.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.920
'Oh, well, try again.'

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.800
Oh, another?

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.120
Oh!

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.200
Next!

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.800
'Oh, hold on!

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.120
'180 million years of history in an instant.'

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.440
I'm quite happy with that.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:44.720
That one's going to be quite nice there.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.320
This one, this one.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.680
And these ones, we can potentially clean up.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.800
So you could clean those up and make them brighter and...?

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.920
Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.320
More attractive?

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.120
I reckon, yeah.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:08:05.720
While Paddy polishes up my fossils, I'm going back 200 years, to when Lyme Regis earned its nickname, Fossil Town.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:12.440
It began with a kind of Indiana Jones in a bonnet - Mary Anning.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:18.920
She scoured the beaches and cliffs, collecting fossils to sell to wealthy summer visitors.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:31.880
In 1811, Mary's brother spotted a skull protruding out of a cliff, and over the following months, Mary proceeded to dig out an almost entire skeleton of an ancient crocodile creature.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.280
She was just 12 years old.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:37.600
Mary sold it for £23 - about £1,000 in today's money.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.080
There is a replica in the Lyme Regis Museum.

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:50.520
The creature was eventually named an ichthyosaur, and this is what it would have looked like 200 million years ago, swimming in Lyme Bay.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:58.360
'Like most geologists, 'Tom Sharpe from Cardiff Museum is a Mary Anning fan.'

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:03.800
Do you think she has genuinely influenced fossil hunters today and geologists today?

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.480
I mean, she was a great tourist attraction in her own right, in her own time.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.120
People came to Lyme Regis to go fossil hunting with Mary Anning.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:15.760
And you look around you today, there are fossil shops here in Lyme Regis, and there are collectors here, carrying on the tradition of Mary Anning.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:19.920
She made some significant discoveries during her time, didn't she?

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.600
Oh, yes, she did.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:26.840
I mean, she really transformed our view of life in the Jurassic sea, 200 million years ago.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.480
She was finding some remarkable creatures, which no-one had ever seen before, and finding complete examples as well.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:38.800
And she was finding these things at just the right time, when geology was becoming established as a science.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.960
So, she was certainly born at the right time, but she was a woman - was she born the right sex?

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.200
No, she wasn't, really.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:47.840
And she wasn't the right class either.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:56.000
We forget, really, how strongly stratified - almost as well stratified as the rocks are round here - British society was 200 years ago.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.440
There was no opportunity for her to move up the social scale.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:07.360
She'd probably be one of the world's leading palaeontologists if she were alive today.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:14.520
She found the first long-necked plesiosaur and a flying dinosaur, the pterodactyl.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:21.840
In 1830, renowned geologist Henry De la Beche imagined the seas and skies populated by Mary's creatures.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:31.480
She became so well known, her work is said to have inspired the tongue twister, "She sells seashells on the seashore".

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:38.640
My fossil may not be up to Mary's standard, but I'm hoping that Paddy's been able to clean it up.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.440
It's the big moment.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.560
This is the one we found this morning on the beach.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:45.920
There's your ammonite.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.000
That is lovely!

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.360
'I'm quite impressed with that.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.920
'But it's small fry,' compared to this.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.680
An ammonite pavement.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:59.080
A fossil graveyard made up of hundreds of squid-like creatures that lived in shells.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.400
Sadly, it's slowly disintegrating.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:05.040
Richard Edmonds from the Jurassic Coast team is trying to find out why.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:15.200
Over the last few years, I've noticed there's been some really weird movements down here, ledges pushing and graunching and breaking.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.880
Why do you think it is shifting so?

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.080
We really don't know at the moment.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.560
It's a bit of a mystery, but we're not seeing it anywhere else along the Jurassic Coast.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.800
And is it something that we should be worried about?

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.880
I mean, this will be gone one day?

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.280
No, I mean, this coast is a product of erosion.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.040
If it wasn't eroding and changing, it wouldn't be the place it is.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:35.920
We may lose quite a lot of this ammonite pavement, but then, some more of it will appear somewhere else, and we've just got to live with that fact.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.080
It's the nature of this coastline.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:52.160
And that's where Richard's team have gone high-tech, and called in Greg Colley with his helicopter camera.

00:11:51.280 --> 00:12:10.120
We got some funding from Natural England to fly this helicopter with a camera at really high resolution to make a photomontage, you know, a baseline survey, so that I can come back and actually see what's actually happening, how this beach is changing through time.
